  <notes-header>

  <chapter*|A stochastic analysis perspective on Euclidean fields>

  <notes-abstract|Lectures at the 53rd Saint-Flour Probability Summer
  School, 30 June--12 July 2025: lecture notes and recordings.>

  <with|font-series|bold|Lecturer.> Massimiliano Gubinelli (University of
  Oxford).

  These eight lectures were given at the <hlink|53rd Saint-Flour Probability
  Summer School|https://lmbp.uca.fr/stflour/lectures.html> (Saint-Flour,
  France), together with the courses of Justin Salez and Peter Bartlett.

  <subsection*|Abstract>

  The goal of these lectures is to introduce Euclidean quantum field theory
  as natural objects in probability theory: higher dimensional
  generalisations of Markov processes. I will explain the motivations and
  problems inherent in a rigorous theory of these objects and indicate how
  it is possible to use the fundamental ideas of stochastic analysis to
  tackle them, at least in dimensions two and three. We will explore various
  ways to construct Euclidean fields by solving certain stochastic analysis
  problems, a method called stochastic quantisation. If time permits I will
  also touch upon the open problem of giving a mathematical
  characterisation of Euclidean fields.

  <subsection*|Lecture notes>

  <with|font-series|bold|A stochastic analysis perspective on Euclidean
  fields.> [<notes-store|PDF|st-flour/2025/euclidean-fields-notes.pdf>]
  [<notes-link|HTML|st-flour-2025/euclidean-fields-notes.tm>]. Gaussian free
  field and Wick powers, the variational approach, the quartic and
  exponential interactions in two dimensions, a scale-by-scale interpolation
  and the quartic interaction in three dimensions.

  <subsection*|Recordings>

  The lectures were recorded by the school; all eight are in the
  <hlink|YouTube playlist|https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL79F_c_jsxZHMFnQdCTP18mu2x3U0hLl6>.
  Each lecture lasts about ninety minutes. The times below link to the start
  of each topic in the recording, and the section numbers refer to the
  lecture notes. All dates are in 2025.

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 1> (Tuesday 1 July): <hlink|Euclidean fields as Markov random fields|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hOc8mdNF_8k>.
    Euclidean fields as perturbations of the Gaussian free field; the problems of renormalisation, large fields and infinite volume; the models and the plan of the course. Notes: Sections 1 and 2.1.

    <\itemize>
      <item><hlink|00:05|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hOc8mdNF_8k&t=5s> Motivation: Euclidean fields as higher-dimensional Markov processes

      <item><hlink|07:25|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hOc8mdNF_8k&t=445s> Formal Gibbs measures and the Markov property

      <item><hlink|14:44|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hOc8mdNF_8k&t=884s> The Gaussian free field as a random distribution

      <item><hlink|27:14|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hOc8mdNF_8k&t=1634s> Renormalisation, large fields and infinite volume

      <item><hlink|40:01|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hOc8mdNF_8k&t=2401s> Regularisation and the models: <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>, <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>, <math|P<around*|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>>, exponential and sine-Gordon

      <item><hlink|53:25|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hOc8mdNF_8k&t=3205s> Plan of the course; questions on locality and on dimension four

      <item><hlink|1:13:16|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hOc8mdNF_8k&t=4396s> Regularity: Hölder and Besov spaces
    </itemize>

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 2> (Wednesday 2 July): <hlink|Besov spaces, the Gaussian free field and Wick powers|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_rFh1MueQn8>.
    Besov spaces, the regularity of the Gaussian free field on the torus, and Wick powers in two dimensions. Notes: Sections 2.1--2.3.

    <\itemize>
      <item><hlink|02:31|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_rFh1MueQn8&t=151s> Littlewood--Paley blocks and Besov spaces

      <item><hlink|13:08|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_rFh1MueQn8&t=788s> Bernstein inequality, embeddings and Fourier multipliers

      <item><hlink|29:39|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_rFh1MueQn8&t=1779s> Regularity of the Gaussian free field on the torus

      <item><hlink|54:38|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_rFh1MueQn8&t=3278s> Local functionals and their regularisation

      <item><hlink|1:05:24|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_rFh1MueQn8&t=3924s> Hermite polynomials, Wick powers and renormalisation constants

      <item><hlink|1:23:36|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_rFh1MueQn8&t=5016s> Regularity of the Wick powers in two dimensions by hypercontractivity
    </itemize>

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 3> (Thursday 3 July): <hlink|Nelson's construction and the Boué--Dupuis formula|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5X-voOdP0A0>.
    Wick powers in three dimensions and in weighted spaces, Nelson's construction of <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>, and the variational approach. Notes: Sections 2.1--2.4 and 3.

    <\itemize>
      <item><hlink|00:06|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5X-voOdP0A0&t=6s> Wick powers, renormalisation and Wiener chaos

      <item><hlink|09:57|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5X-voOdP0A0&t=597s> Wick powers in three dimensions

      <item><hlink|19:06|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5X-voOdP0A0&t=1146s> Weighted Besov spaces and Wick powers on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>

      <item><hlink|35:35|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5X-voOdP0A0&t=2135s> Nelson's construction of <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>

      <item><hlink|1:03:24|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5X-voOdP0A0&t=3804s> The Gibbs variational principle

      <item><hlink|1:10:45|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5X-voOdP0A0&t=4245s> Relative entropy on Wiener space, Föllmer drift and Girsanov theorem

      <item><hlink|1:21:21|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5X-voOdP0A0&t=4881s> The Boué--Dupuis formula; an example with Lipschitz functionals
    </itemize>

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 4> (Saturday 5 July): <hlink|Variational bounds for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIEQxMTvCKg>.
    The martingale embedding of the Gaussian free field and bounds on the variational problem for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> that are uniform in the cutoff; first steps in infinite volume. Notes: Sections 3.2 and 4.

    <\itemize>
      <item><hlink|00:05|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIEQxMTvCKg&t=5s> The Gibbs variational principle and the Girsanov density

      <item><hlink|07:31|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIEQxMTvCKg&t=451s> Weak formulation and Boué--Dupuis controls

      <item><hlink|20:37|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIEQxMTvCKg&t=1237s> Martingale embedding of the Gaussian free field

      <item><hlink|30:27|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIEQxMTvCKg&t=1827s> The Boué--Dupuis formula for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>

      <item><hlink|44:22|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIEQxMTvCKg&t=2662s> Shifted Wick powers and the coercive term

      <item><hlink|50:58|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIEQxMTvCKg&t=3058s> Pathwise estimates uniform in the cutoff

      <item><hlink|1:15:24|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIEQxMTvCKg&t=4524s> Infinite volume and the Euler--Lagrange equation
    </itemize>

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 5> (Tuesday 8 July): <hlink|<math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> in infinite volume|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-ZkfH2K_dg>.
    Construction of <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> from a priori estimates on the Euler--Lagrange equation that do not depend on the volume; beginning of large deviations. Notes: Sections 4 and 4.1.

    <\itemize>
      <item><hlink|00:05|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-ZkfH2K_dg&t=5s> Volume cutoff and tightness

      <item><hlink|06:42|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-ZkfH2K_dg&t=402s> Uniqueness and phase transitions

      <item><hlink|10:01|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-ZkfH2K_dg&t=601s> The Euler--Lagrange equation as a forward--backward equation; open problems

      <item><hlink|17:27|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-ZkfH2K_dg&t=1047s> Minimisers and a coupling of <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> with the Gaussian free field

      <item><hlink|35:24|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-ZkfH2K_dg&t=2124s> Weighted energy estimates uniform in the volume

      <item><hlink|1:03:34|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-ZkfH2K_dg&t=3814s> Tightness and the equation in infinite volume

      <item><hlink|1:17:30|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-ZkfH2K_dg&t=4650s> Large deviations in the semiclassical limit
    </itemize>

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 6> (Wednesday 9 July): <hlink|Large deviations and exponential integrability|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3YZfBqjju5E>.
    The large deviation principle for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>, exponential functionals through perturbed variational problems, and the exponential interaction. Notes: Sections 3.1, 4.1 and 5.

    <\itemize>
      <item><hlink|00:05|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3YZfBqjju5E&t=5s> Large deviations as a semiclassical limit

      <item><hlink|08:21|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3YZfBqjju5E&t=501s> The small-noise forward--backward equation

      <item><hlink|23:09|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3YZfBqjju5E&t=1389s> The limiting elliptic equation and uniqueness for convex functionals

      <item><hlink|33:02|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3YZfBqjju5E&t=1982s> Exponential functionals through perturbed variational problems

      <item><hlink|47:21|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3YZfBqjju5E&t=2841s> Exponential integrability of <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>

      <item><hlink|59:07|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3YZfBqjju5E&t=3547s> Completion of the large deviation principle; discussion

      <item><hlink|1:10:55|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3YZfBqjju5E&t=4255s> The exponential model and Gaussian multiplicative chaos
    </itemize>

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 7> (Thursday 10 July): <hlink|The exponential interaction; <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M>.
    Construction and uniqueness of the exponential model in infinite volume; set-up of the variational problem for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on the torus. Notes: Sections 5, 6 and 7.

    <\itemize>
      <item><hlink|00:56|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M&t=56s> Gaussian multiplicative chaos

      <item><hlink|04:20|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M&t=260s> The exponential model and its variational formula

      <item><hlink|14:50|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M&t=890s> The Euler--Lagrange equation, the sign of the drift and infinite volume

      <item><hlink|28:12|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M&t=1692s> Uniqueness by convexity

      <item><hlink|40:28|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M&t=2428s> Why the argument fails for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>; a remark on sine-Gordon

      <item><hlink|51:09|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M&t=3069s> <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>: scale-by-scale decomposition of the Gaussian free field

      <item><hlink|58:36|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M&t=3516s> The Boué--Dupuis functional for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> and its counterterms

      <item><hlink|1:12:33|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uvP7btwWC8M&t=4353s> Completion of the square and change of control
    </itemize>

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 8> (Saturday 12 July): <hlink|Paraproducts and renormalisation of <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs>.
    Paraproducts and commutator estimates, and the second renormalisation of <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>. Notes: Sections 7, 7.1 and 7.2.

    <\itemize>
      <item><hlink|00:05|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs&t=5s> Recap of the variational problem for <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>

      <item><hlink|05:04|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs&t=304s> The new control and the regularity of the drift

      <item><hlink|28:55|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs&t=1735s> Paraproducts and resonant products

      <item><hlink|49:27|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs&t=2967s> Duality and commutator lemmas

      <item><hlink|58:53|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs&t=3533s> Commutators with Fourier multipliers

      <item><hlink|1:03:46|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs&t=3826s> Decomposition of the quadratic term

      <item><hlink|1:21:05|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs&t=4865s> The renormalised product and the logarithmic counterterm

      <item><hlink|1:29:17|https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=646jvCo6gPs&t=5357s> Second completion of the square; outlook
    </itemize>
  </itemize>
